\begin{abstract}
Quantum computers are prone to errors during qubit measurements, yet these noisy results can be mitigated using machine learning. This paper presents a new mitigation method, the Quantum Tomographic Autoencoder (QTAE), which trains a deep neural network on noisy measurements from a specific quantum computer. Unlike prior memoryless or Markovian models, QTAE captures long-range correlations in error patterns, offering a more comprehensive model. We validate QTAE's efficacy via experiments on an IBM qubit processor, where it outperformed both memoryless and Markovian approaches across all configurations. Furthermore, QTAE scales more effectively than its competitors as circuit depth increases. These results confirm that QTAE is a robust, machine-agnostic solution for readout error correction on IBM qubit systems.
\end{abstract}

