 essential for numerous clinical applications, including spinal deformity analysis, fracture detection, and surgical planning. However, automatic vertebral localization and identification frequently fail in real-world scenarios due to the presence of artifacts, such as contrast media, air, and surgical instruments. This paper introduces a novel method to mitigate these issues by employing a multi-scale attention mechanism integrated with a convolutional neural network. This mechanism selectively emphasizes relevant regions within the input image, thereby enhancing robustness against contrast media and other anomalies. The proposed approach was rigorously validated using a dataset of 1,000 coronal digital tomosynthesis spine images. Experimental results demonstrate that the method significantly reduces the average localization error from 1.87 to 1.39 vertebrae and improves identification accuracy from 61.4% to 68.2%. Furthermore, the study presents a new dataset and associated software tools to facilitate future research in vertical spine assessment via digital tomosynthesis. The source code is publicly accessible at https://github.com/ChengWang-2000/AVERT. The authors declare no conflicts of interest. \begin{center} \textbf{Keywords:} Spinal Deformity Assessment; Digital Tomosynthesis Synthesis; Vertebrae Localization and Identification; Multi-Scale Attention Mechanism \end{center} \noindent \textbf{Acknowledgments} \par This research was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant No. 81971777), the Beijing Natural Science Foundation (Grant No. 7202174), and the Fundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities (Grant No. 2021JBZ009). The authors gratefully acknowledge these funding agencies.
